VULNERABLE.

Competing hypotheses: (1) integer overflow in the size computations `SVGA_BITMAP_SIZE(x, y)` / `SVGA_PIXMAP_SIZE(x, y, cursor.bpp)` feeding the loop bounds; (2) missing FIFO-length validation before the bulk reads in the `SVGA_CMD_DEFINE_CURSOR` case. Hypothesis 1 is largely mitigated by the explicit checks `cursor.width > 256 || cursor.height > 256 || cursor.bpp > 32 || SVGA_BITMAP_SIZE(x, y) > sizeof cursor.mask || SVGA_PIXMAP_SIZE(x, y, cursor.bpp) > sizeof cursor.image`, which bound the loop counts to the size of the local `cursor` struct. Hypothesis 2 is the real path.

Chosen path: attacker-controlled FIFO contents → missing length check → out-of-bounds FIFO reads.

The attacker, via guest-written FIFO memory, issues `SVGA_CMD_DEFINE_CURSOR` and supplies `cursor.width`, `cursor.height`, and `cursor.bpp` (all read with `vmsvga_fifo_read(s)` from attacker-controlled FIFO data). The code computes:

```c
args = SVGA_BITMAP_SIZE(x, y) + SVGA_PIXMAP_SIZE(x, y, cursor.bpp);
```

and validates the sizes against the `cursor` struct, but never subtracts `args` from `len` and never checks `len < 0` before consuming the payload — unlike every other command, which does `len -= N; if (len < 0) goto rewind;`. Instead, immediately after the size check, control falls into:

```c
for (args = 0; args < SVGA_BITMAP_SIZE(x, y); args++) {
    cursor.mask[args] = vmsvga_fifo_read_raw(s);
}
for (args = 0; args < SVGA_PIXMAP_SIZE(x, y, cursor.bpp); args++) {
    cursor.image[args] = vmsvga_fifo_read_raw(s);
}
```

The sink is `vmsvga_fifo_read_raw(s)`, executed up to `SVGA_BITMAP_SIZE(x, y) + SVGA_PIXMAP_SIZE(x, y, cursor.bpp)` times with no check that that many words actually remain in the FIFO (`len` is not consulted at all on this path). With the maximum allowed dimensions (256×256, bpp up to 32), the number of reads far exceeds the FIFO contents the guest actually published, so the loop reads past the valid FIFO region — an out-of-bounds read reachable directly from attacker input, and it also desynchronizes `fifo_stop` from the real data end. The `goto rewind;` that appears right after the size check short-circuits the intended flow rather than guarding the reads, so no length accounting protects the sink.

The other cases (UPDATE, RECT_FILL, RECT_COPY, badcmd handling) all perform their `len -= N; if (len < 0) goto rewind;` accounting before reading, so they are not on the critical path.

